1. [MAJOR] SURVEY.md:10-11 — The "re-anchored to the tree that now ships" `src/stdlib.rs` citations are stale by exactly +4 lines after the resume edit to `src/stdlib.rs:1108-1135`. The file claims `BUILTIN_NAMES` is at `src/stdlib.rs:1855` and `"input"` at `1975`, but on the current tree the const is at `src/stdlib.rs:1859` and `"input"` is at `src/stdlib.rs:1979`; `regex_builtin` is at 1534 (claimed 1530), the `split` doc at 2025 (claimed 2021), `to_text` at ~2180 (claimed 2176), and `json.stringify` at ~2304 (claimed 2300). The survey's own status line says every claim carries "the line that proves it," and the re-verification paragraph (SURVEY.md:~419-424) says `tests/stdlib_gap_survey_test.rs` "refuses one that does not resolve." Either that test is not content-checking the citation, or the uncommitted REPORT's claim of a green `cargo test --all-targets` on the resume tree is false. Fix: add 4 to every `src/stdlib.rs` citation after line 1109, or make the survey test open the cited line and assert it contains the claimed symbol. VERIFIED: read SURVEY.md:10-11 and `src/stdlib.rs:1859`/`1979`; the +4 offset matches the uncommitted net +4 insertion at `src/stdlib.rs:1108-1135`.

2. [MAJOR] src/stdlib.rs:1460-1463 — `padded_text` reserves `(left + right) * fill.len()` bytes, but the loops at `src/stdlib.rs:1471-1477` emit at most `left + right` *characters* cycled from `fill`. `fill.len()` is the byte length of the whole fill, not the width of one fill character, so a large multi-character fill amplifies the reservation without bound: `text.pad("", 1000, <1 MB fill>)` reserves ~1 GB (and `width = 1_000_000` reserves ~1 TB and is refused) while the real output is only ~1 KB. A valid call can therefore fail with "the padded text is too large to allocate," or allocate orders of magnitude more than it uses. No test covers a fill longer than a couple of characters. Fix: bound the reservation by the actual maximum byte width per emitted character, e.g. `(left + right) * fill.chars().map(char::len_utf8).max().unwrap_or(0) + text.len()`, or reserve `(left + right) * 4 + text.len()`. VERIFIED: traced `src/stdlib.rs:1460-1477`; `take(left)`/`take(right)` bound emitted characters, never `fill.len()` characters.

3. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:421-426 — The six module-qualified names (`text_wrap`, `text_diff`, `text_dedent`, `text_template`, `text_pad`, `text_align`) are inserted into the global builtin table here and listed in `BUILTIN_NAMES` at `src/stdlib.rs:1880-1885`, so a bare identifier call such as `text_wrap("x", 3)` resolves as a builtin (the `resolve` comment at `src/stdlib.rs:1829-1830` returns the name unchanged for names already in `BUILTIN_NAMES`). This contradicts SPEC.md's new paragraph "These six are spelled as module members only" and the REPORT's "reachable only through the `text` module." Fix: either document/accept the internal names as ordinary builtins and correct the SPEC wording, or gate the dispatcher on the invocation having been dotted; add a test for the bare spelling. (Did not run a REPL check; the registry path makes the reachability clear, but a `say text_wrap("x", 3)` program would settle it.)

